-Every thermally important selected active shell satisfies $\cS_a^{\rm act}=1$ to machine precision in the present validation. This equality is symmetry enforced in the model rather than a numerical accident. The second-order Kane Hamiltonian used here omits explicit bulk-inversion-asymmetry (BIA) terms. In that approximation, inversion $P$ and spinful time reversal $T$ produce a fixed-$\mathbf k$ antiunitary $PT$ doublet. Because velocity is odd under both $P$ and $T$, it is $PT$ even. In $PT$-adapted doublet bases the selected velocity block has quaternionic form
+Every thermally important selected active shell satisfies $\cS_a^{\rm act}=1$ to machine precision in the present validation. The relevant qualification is dimensional. In the present numerical domain, each thermally relevant selected \emph{parent} exact shell is a single fixed-$\mathbf k$ $PT$ Kramers doublet. The second-order Kane Hamiltonian used here omits explicit bulk-inversion-asymmetry (BIA) terms, so inversion $P$ and spinful time reversal $T$ generate that fixed-$\mathbf k$ doublet. Because velocity is odd under both $P$ and $T$, it is $PT$ even. For one two-dimensional parent doublet coupling to one partner doublet, the $PT$-adapted velocity block has quaternionic form
 \begin{equation}
-M=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\-b^*&a^*\end{pmatrix},
+M_j=\begin{pmatrix}a_j&b_j\\-b_j^*&a_j^*\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad
-MM^\dagger=(|a|^2+|b|^2)I_2,
+M_jM_j^\dagger=(|a_j|^2+|b_j|^2)I_2.
 \label{eq:quaternion}
 \end{equation}
-which forces equal nonzero singular values. Concatenating partner doublets preserves Eq.~\eqref{eq:quaternion}.
+If that same parent doublet couples to several partner doublets, horizontal concatenation gives $MM^\dagger=\sum_jM_jM_j^\dagger\propto I_2$, so the two nonzero singular values remain equal. By contrast, $PT$ symmetry for a general multidoublet parent block guarantees Kramers-paired singular values but does \emph{not} require all nonzero singular values to be equal.
 
